IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `sarca-licensing`
IMPORT `sarca-qa`

-- Security and Related Activities (Control) Act 1996 (WA) -- cases for the Q&A layer (sarca-qa.l4): every exported
-- question, for every role and every situation.
--
-- Each question is asked for the two roles it is not about, with every other input an ASSUMEd value that has none: the
-- answer must be `does not arise for you`, and reading any of those inputs would stop the evaluation with an error ("it
-- is an assumed term"). Each question that takes a situation input is asked, for its own role, with each value under
-- which the situation does not hold and every later input ASSUMEd: again `does not arise for you`. For the security
-- officer (the baseline's seat) each answer is also asserted equal to the baseline export's own answer on the same
-- facts. Expected values are worked by hand from the deposited text, never copied from the output.
--
-- Facts: Jake (k060), born 30 August 2006, joining Sentinel Security Services, and Daniel's tower (d04b), as in the
-- baseline's cases (sarca-cases-licensing.l4), whose fixtures are restated here; the rest are the encoder's
-- illustrations.
--
-- BASELINE. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Cases -- the Q&A layer`

ASSUME `an unasked fact` IS A BOOLEAN
ASSUME `an unasked number` IS A NUMBER
ASSUME `an unasked date` IS A DATE
ASSUME `unasked work` IS A `My work`
ASSUME `an unasked licence` IS A `Licence type`
ASSUME `an unasked matter` IS A `My past matter`
ASSUME `unasked matters` IS A LIST OF `My past matter`
ASSUME `an unasked application` IS A `My application`
ASSUME `an unasked task` IS AN `Armed task`
ASSUME `an unasked fee item` IS A `Fee item`
ASSUME `an unasked ground` IS A `Ground against renewal`
ASSUME `an unasked asker` IS A `Who is asking`
ASSUME `an unasked event` IS A `What happened to me`
ASSUME `an unasked conclusion` IS A `What the Commissioner concludes`
ASSUME `an unasked decision` IS A `Licensing decision`
ASSUME `an unasked offence` IS A `My offence`
ASSUME `an unasked business offence` IS A `My business's offence`
ASSUME `an unasked way of getting security` IS A `How I get security for my property`

`as the officer` MEANS `I work, or want to work, as a security officer`
`as the security business` MEANS `I run a business that supplies security officers to others`
`as the client` MEANS `I hire or employ security for my own property`

`employing my own guard` MEANS `I employ my own guard`
`hiring a firm` MEANS `I hire a security firm to supply guards`

`Jake guarding the tower for Sentinel` MEANS `My work` WITH
    `I am paid for it` IS TRUE
    `I watch, guard or protect property` IS TRUE
    `my one employer, not a security agent, employs me to guard its own property` IS FALSE
    `I carry a firearm or baton while working` IS FALSE
    `I escort a person as a guard or protector` IS FALSE
    `I advise on watching, guarding or protecting property` IS FALSE
    `I install, maintain or repair security equipment listed in s 14` IS FALSE
    `the equipment is in a vehicle, vessel or aircraft` IS FALSE
    `as a regular duty I control, screen or remove people at a venue or event` IS FALSE
    `I investigate people's conduct or character, or look for missing persons` IS FALSE
    `I supply other people to do security work` IS FALSE
    `I am on official duty as a police officer or in the Defence Force` IS FALSE
    `I am on official duty as a public officer` IS FALSE
    `I am a court security, custodial or prison services contract worker doing that work` IS FALSE
    `I hold a temporary licence covering this work` IS FALSE

`a guard employed by Jarrah to watch its own office` MEANS `My work` WITH
